  "abstract": "Let a double cone be the graph obtained by adjoining two adjacent universal vertices to a finite simple graph. We prove that, if the double cone is biplanar and its body has twenty-one vertices and independence number at most three, then the body partitions into seven independent triples. Consequently its complement has a triangle factor and the full graph has chromatic number nine. Earlier bounds provided six disjoint triples and a choice of any prescribed singleton in the thirteen nonneighbors of a degree-seven vertex. To extend to seven triples, we fix such a vertex and use matching inequalities to bound the contacts between its neighbors and nonneighbors. Complete isomorphism inventories leave 115 possible induced graphs on the thirteen nonneighbors and 5,608 pairs of fixed graphs to consider. For each pair, every possible edge between the two sides remains a variable. A completion that destroys a specified partition must contain an edge internal to one of its classes. The supplement gives 482,050 explicit partitions, the checks of the vertex labels and fixed edges, and the independently verifiable propositional refutations obtained from these constraints. Together with the earlier order bounds, the theorem leaves exactly twenty body vertices for a ten-chromatic double cone in this independence class.",
